= Entity — Technical =

== Domain of discourse ==
In formal semantics, an interpretation normally includes a domain D of objects/entities over which variables range.

A term may denote an element of D.

Which objects are admitted to D reflects the modeling language and ontological commitments.

== Individual / class separation ==
Many KR systems distinguish:
* individuals;
* classes;
* properties/relations.

But the metalanguage may itself treat classes and properties as entities.

RDF, for example, permits resources to function in several representational roles, while OWL DL places restrictions on how vocabularies can be used to preserve formal properties.

== Entity resolution ==
Given records r1 and r2, entity resolution estimates whether:

<pre>
denotes(r1) = denotes(r2)
</pre>

Evidence can include identifiers and attribute similarity.

Probabilistic linkage produces uncertainty over identity; it does not by itself justify a hard equality assertion.

== Persistent identity ==
Tracking an entity through time requires criteria for persistence.

The criterion can depend on entity type:
* organism;
* legal organization;
* software release;
* vessel;
* dataset.

Version changes may preserve identity for one purpose and create a new entity for another.

== Referential opacity ==
Substitution of co-referring expressions can fail in belief/intensional contexts.

A person may believe that "the Morning Star is visible" without knowing that the Morning Star is Venus.

Thus entity identity does not erase semantic differences among referring expressions.

